A change of packages/fcl-passrc/src/pasresolver.pp of Free Pascal 3.2.2 (tag release_3_2_2), applied by get.sh.
Licence of this patch: that of the patched file, the LGPL with the static linking exception of Free Pascal (COPYING.FPC
in the FPC sources); see README.md.

1. TPasResolver.CheckUseAsType: in ObjFPC the bare name of a generic is allowed inside the implementation of its own
   methods too (class function TOwned.Own(AObj: T): TOwned), as FPC allows it.
2. The for-in enumerator may be a record, as FPC allows (for V in Ch over TChan<T>).
3. A class operator of a record is not static, and its implementation is found by the operator name.
4. ObjFPC passes a typed pointer to a var or out argument of type Pointer (GetMem(P, Size)), as FPC does.
5. A pointer to a member type of a generic declared after it (PData = ^TData) is specialized once all members are.
6. InterfaceOnly (a used unit read up to its implementation): no section, method bodies or generic bodies are required.
7. String[N] adds no identifier "string" to the scope: its string type is a sub type of the alias (A = String[3]), so
   two of them in one unit are not a duplicate and a later "string" still names the type of the system unit.
8. String is AnsiString under {$H+} (and Char is AnsiChar): array of String and array of AnsiString are one type
   (CheckElTypeCompatibility), and a String variable is accepted for a var or out AnsiString argument
   (RunCommand of the Process unit takes one).
9. TPasResolver.IsCharLiteral: a second # (#13#10) or another character that is no digit or letter ends the scan: the
   literal is a string, not a char, so Pos(#13#10, S) does not match the Char overload too.
10. Insert and Delete take a string variable as well as a dynamic array (Delete(S, 1, 1)); FPC has them as compiler
    procedures, so the stubs of the RTL do not declare them.
11. S[1] of a string variable can be passed to a var argument (Sock.Read(S[1], N), FillChar(S[1], N, 0)); the resolver
    marks it assignable but not writable.
12. A procedure whose calling convention is the default fits a procedure type with a declared one (and the other way
    round): {$calling cdecl} is not read, and FPC itself checks the convention.
13. A typecast of a value above the Int64 range (QWord($CBF29CE484222325)) is evaluated (EvalBaseTypeCast): QWord keeps
    it, Int64 takes its bits, other types are not folded; it used to stop with "not yet implemented".
14. An element of a typed pointer is a variable (B[I] := X with B: PByte, PByte(P)[I] := X; pointer math of ObjFPC):
    TPasResolver.CheckCanBeLHS took only P^ for a writable element without an identifier and stopped with "identifier
    expected, but Byte found" (unxed/bp#203).

